    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0032",
      "speaker_id": "person_2",
      "role": "DOCTOR",
      "start_seconds": 241.68,
      "end_seconds": 247.49,
      "duration_seconds": 5.81,
      "text": "The eczema patches on the arms have yellow crusts and weeping areas."
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0033",
      "speaker_id": "person_1",
      "role": "PATIENT",
      "start_seconds": 247.99,
      "end_seconds": 248.89,
      "duration_seconds": 0.9,
      "text": "Of course."
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0034",
      "speaker_id": "person_2",
      "role": "DOCTOR",
      "start_seconds": 251.87,
      "end_seconds": 253.85,
      "duration_seconds": 1.98,
      "text": "Okay that's enough for me."
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0035",
      "speaker_id": "person_2",
      "role": "DOCTOR",
      "start_seconds": 263.49,
      "end_seconds": 276.4,
      "duration_seconds": 12.91,
      "text": "Let me tell you where I've got to. The The most likely explanation is eczema with a bacterial skin infection. I can't rule out herpes simplex eczema yet."
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0036",
      "speaker_id": "person_2",
      "role": "DOCTOR",
      "start_seconds": 290.86,
      "end_seconds": 295.3,
      "duration_seconds": 4.44,
      "text": "A skin swab is what I'd like next, for infected eczema"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0037",
      "speaker_id": "person_1",
      "role": "PATIENT",
      "start_seconds": 295.92,
      "end_seconds": 297.28,
      "duration_seconds": 1.36,
      "text": "does it hurt?"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0038",
      "speaker_id": "person_2",
      "role": "DOCTOR",
      "start_seconds": 298.32,
      "end_seconds": 325.48,
      "duration_seconds": 27.16,
      "text": "We'll begin with flucloxacillin, 250 milligrams, four times a day, seven days. That should help with infected eczema. Keep the nails short and keep using the moisturiser. And please bring the latest prescription or the medicine boxes to the next visit, so we can settle the dose. Do get help quickly if the symptoms get worse. We'll catch up in one week."
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0039",
      "speaker_id": "person_2",
      "role": "DOCTOR",
      "start_seconds": 326.64,
      "end_seconds": 335.35,
      "duration_seconds": 8.71,
      "text": "Alright, can you tell me the plan back in your own words, so I know I've explained it clearly?"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0040",
      "speaker_id": "person_1",
      "role": "PATIENT",
      "start_seconds": 337.09,
      "end_seconds": 346.44,
      "duration_seconds": 9.35,
      "text": "so I take the flucloxacillin 250 milligrams four times a day and come back in one week is that right?"
    },
